            <div class="step2-reading">
                <h2 class="step-title"><?php echo $_GET['archetype'];?>'s Unique Contribution</h2>
                <img class="img-intext" src="/offer/paa/img/mandala-1.png">
                <p>The Outlaw Archetype is one of the three archetypes who desire nothing more but to leave a mark on the world, through the act of liberation.&nbsp;</p>
                <p>Liberation is the Outlaws&rsquo; unique contribution to the world, and it&rsquo;s also the barometer for how The Outlaw Archetype makes sense of the world:</p>
                <p>To the Outlaw, life is not worth living if you can&rsquo;t be free from all limitation.</p>
                <p>Outlaws do not yearn to make their mark on the world through mastery as the Hero does. Nor do they wish to attain power like the Magician (although it might seem that way occasionally to others).To the Outlaw, achieving total liberation for themselves and everyone else is the only way to do things.</p>
                <p><strong>Disrupting The Status Quo</strong></p>
                <p>True to their beliefs, Outlaws are almost always known for challenging the status quo in most situations they experience. Such can frequently be confusing and frustrating to others around them, especially if people who surround the Outlaw don&rsquo;t understand why Outlaws are always pushing boundaries.&nbsp;</p>
                <p><strong>The Fight For Freedom Is Endless</strong></p>
                <p>Outlaws are continually pushing and fighting for freedom, not just for themselves but for everybody.However, the sense of freedom they fight for depends on their personal beliefs, morals, conditioning, and understanding of the world - which means that each Outlaw might have a very different fight inside them.&nbsp;</p>
                <p>In other words, each fight an Outlaw undertakes (and there will be many) will usually always be a fight for fairness and sustainable living, but the notion of what is fair and sustainable will be unique to each Outlaw's perspective (and not necessarily always right).&nbsp;</p>
                <p><strong>Totally Dedicated</strong></p>
                <p>It&rsquo;s safe to say that the Outlaw is committed to a fault to their plight (unless they&rsquo;ve learned how to adjust their behavior and beliefs to conform socially through parental and social conditioning).However, as admirable and dedicated that might sound it doesn&rsquo;t always serve them well - as we will come to see.</p>
                <p>There&rsquo;s not too much in the way that we live our life that the Outlaw will easily sanction.And in some cases, it&rsquo;s easy to see why - this world is crazy and not conducive to fairness and sustainable living in any way, which can activate an Outlaw immensely.&nbsp;</p>
                <p>The trouble is, the Outlaw needs to learn to organize themselves, their thoughts and intentions and then pick their fights to make a real difference and they also need to know when to stop.&nbsp;</p>
                <p>There are times when too much challenging leads to others switching off from the Outlaw&rsquo;s plight even if they might agree with them, or have the power to make the changes.Picking their fights is a difficult thing for the Outlaw to do, as you will see as you read on.&nbsp;</p>
                <p>If you are an Outlaw reading this, you might even feel challenged or outraged by us even mentioning that you occasionally need to slow down and pick your fights.&nbsp;</p>
                <p>If that&rsquo;s you, dear Outlaw, please stay with us.We are about to show you how you can attain real freedom, in the way that you want it, but without some of the limitations that you are always fighting against.</p>
                <div class="prev-next">
                    <div class="container">
                        <div class="row mx-auto col-reverse">
                            <div class="col-sm-6 mx-auto">
                                <button class="btn step1-back hover prev f-left">1. Introduction</button>
                            </div>
                            <div class="col-sm-6">
                                <button class="btn step3 hover f-right">3. Sense of Significance</button>
                            </div>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>
            <div class="step3-reading">
                <h2 class="step-title"><?php echo $_GET['archetype'];?>'s Sense of Significance</h2>
                <img class="img-intext" src="/offer/paa/img/sun-glow.png" style="filter:none">
                <p>Attaining a sense of significance is a vital need for everybody.If we don&rsquo;t figure out a way to feel significant we can start to become stagnant, we might develop poor confidence, and our wellbeing begins to deplete.&nbsp;</p>
                <p>Most people naturally figure out a way to develop their sense of significance, and in the case of The Outlaw Archetype, if they are lucky enough to attain a sense of significance, they will do it through setting themselves and others free.&nbsp;</p>
                <p>If an Outlaw cannot find a way to feel free or doesn&rsquo;t at the very least have their attention on a project that they believe to be the key to freedom they won&rsquo;t be feeling significant at all.Not to themselves or others.</p>
                <p><strong>It&rsquo;s A Vicious Cycle</strong></p>
                <p>Let's remember that the Outlaws primary focus is to change the world through the act of liberation which is why they do the things they do. Unfortunately, though, the rebellious and chaotic way that an Outlaw generally goes about getting things done often causes them to feel constantly shut down and blocked.&nbsp;</p>
                <p>When this occurs, the Outlaw can become frustrated, even angry or too pushy with their opinions and beliefs which doesn&rsquo;t get the results the Outlaw desires and only eats up the Outlaw inside - thus preventing the Outlaw from achieving their goals.</p>
                <p>This, in turn, can cause the Outlaw to turn-in on themselves, or project anger outwardly to anybody and everybody. They might become even more chaotic which closes more doors and leaves the Outlaw feeling even more constrained than they felt in the first place.&nbsp;</p>
                <p>It&rsquo;s a downward cycle to nowhere good, and unfortunately, most Outlaws at some point in their life, if not most of their life, will relate to this experience.&nbsp;</p>
                <p>It&rsquo;s tough for the Outlaw to attain a sense of significance, which is unfortunate because their intentions are usually excellent and extremely compassionate to the world around them.&nbsp;</p>
                <p>The biggest issue here is that the Outlaw cannot abide any form of restriction, even their own limitations.But to achieve anything useful, you have to create order out of chaos and then implement disciplined and focused actions toward attaining goals.It&rsquo;s unfortunate that the very notion of disciplined action can be incredibly repellant to an Outlaw.&nbsp;</p>
                <p>If the Outlaw could learn that there is more than one way to do things and permit themselves to practice patience and discipline for the higher goal, then the story could be entirely different, and they could really shake up the world.&nbsp;</p>
                <p>If an Outlaw accomplishes this challenge, it will give them a sense of significance beyond their own ability to comprehend.&nbsp;</p>
                <p>Outlaws have so much to give to the world if only they would learn to play the long game.</p>
                <div class="prev-next">
                    <div class="container">
                        <div class="row mx-auto col-reverse">
                            <div class="col-sm-6 mx-auto">
                                <button class="btn step2-back hover f-left">2. Unique Contribution</button>
                            </div>
                            <div class="col-sm-6">
                                <button class="btn step4 hover f-right">4. Personality Traits</button>
                            </div>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>
            <div class="step4-reading">
                <h2 class="step-title"><?php echo $_GET['archetype'];?>'s Personality Traits</h2>
                <img class="img-intext" src="/offer/paa/img/floral.png">
                <p>The Outlaw archetype is the typical rule breaker; they are usually extremely challenging and often shake up the world by pointing out everything that is wrong with it.</p>
                <p>Outlaws don&rsquo;t challenge things for the sake of it, although it might seem that way to others. They have an innate disdain for any structure that will limit their freedom or the freedoms of others.They are usually aware of all of the wrongs and unfairness in the world.</p>
                <p><strong>Outlaws Have An Admirable Sense Of Courage</strong></p>
                <p>Outlaws are incredibly courageous and often cynical and outspoken too.But the intentions behind their actions usually are for the greater good.They don&rsquo;t shout out about things that they deem to be fair.Instead, they choose to focus on the things that they feel are unfair - even if they are mistaken occasionally.</p>
                <p>The trouble is, some things have to be a certain way to maintain order, and what is fair to one person may not be fair to another.&nbsp;</p>
                <p><strong>Conflicted and Frustrated</strong></p>
                <p>Outlaws can be extremely conflicted and trapped in a cycle of frustration which can tarnish their experience of life.It often means that not many people get close enough to an Outlaw to see their deep passion and warm heart because it&rsquo;s often shrouded by a cloak of cynicism that other people might move away from.However, those that do get close to an Outlaw, will be blessed to see the kindness and warmth that lies underneath all of the challenging and fight that the Outlaw displays.&nbsp;</p>
                <p><strong>Game Changer</strong></p>
                <p>The Outlaw has the potential to be a real game changer and also to manage to have a fun and fulfilling life with the people that mean the most to them.But to step into their power, Outlaws first need to sort out the issues that they have that hold them back.&nbsp;</p>
                <p><strong>Why Do We Hide Ourselves Even From Ourselves?</strong></p>
                <p>Every person has a hidden side.Jung calls it a shadow aspect, and it&rsquo;s discussed in great detail in our Premium Archetype Reports.&nbsp;</p>
                <p>Here at Individuologist.com we realize that there is a hidden side or an unconscious world (to be more precise) that we experience internally, but we don&rsquo;t, or can't show or express this existence outwardly.It&rsquo;s this hidden aspect that can bring about great reward if we were to focus our attention on it. It&rsquo;s also crucial for helping each different archetype overcome their greatest challenge.&nbsp;</p>
                <p>Before we get started, it&rsquo;s important to acknowledge that there&rsquo;s also a hidden part of us that remains unconscious even to ourselves too and the process of individuation can help us to uncover these hidden aspects of our &lsquo;self&rsquo; too.&nbsp;</p>
                <p>When we start to uncover these &lsquo;hidden&rsquo; aspects of our &lsquo;self,&rsquo; we begin to learn about what is really driving us.We discover the reasons why we do the things we do (or don&rsquo;t do the things that we want to do, or should do).This gives us the opportunity to start to consciously reconcile these hidden aspects so that we can achieve our deepest desires, or reach our fullest potential.&nbsp;</p>
                <p>The Outlaw tends to have a lot of hidden aspects lurking around in their shadow side. Some are consequences of the contradictions that their greatest desire brings to them, which are almost always linked to their behavioral patterns and belief systems fighting against what needs to be done.You see, Outlaws can fight against themselves, even if they don&rsquo;t realize they do.</p>
                <p><strong>Outlaws Are Commonly Angry or Frustrated</strong></p>
                <p>While it might be easy for others to see this pattern, there will be times when the Outlaw secretly feels angry, and frustrated, although it will be hidden somewhere in the Outlaw&rsquo;s psyche.It&rsquo;s likely to be present unless the Outlaw has managed to find a way to express themselves and to attain some success in their efforts to attain freedom.</p>
                <p>It&rsquo;s easy to see why an Outlaw can become angry or frustrated.Outlaws often don&rsquo;t manage to succeed in their projects because they need to limit themselves to achieve their goals (but they don&rsquo;t like limitation). They see the world as being unfair (and in many cases they are right), but they can&rsquo;t always resolve these issues because they don&rsquo;t like having to follow limitations to do so.</p>
                <p>Outlaws probably don&rsquo;t understand why others don't do anything about things that are clearly wrong in the world. They also have problems in organizing their thoughts and disciplining their actions because they desire freedom so much that this can lead to difficulties in towing necessary lines and finding a way of expressing themselves that gets results.&nbsp;</p>
                <p>Combine all of the above with the intense passion and compassion that an Outlaw has, and you can see that&rsquo;s a potent mix that can only turn into some form of frustration or rage.&nbsp;</p>
                <p>The secret world of an Outlaw is intense - more intense than most other archetypes!&nbsp;</p>
                <p><strong>Outlaws Can't Accept That to Create Change There Has To Be Discipline</strong></p>
                <p>One of the most significant issues for an Outlaw is that to create change there needs to be limitation and limitation is the Outlaw&rsquo;s greatest enemy. So, they often won&rsquo;t limit themselves and as a result don&rsquo;t get things done.It&rsquo;s a vicious circle that the Outlaw doesn&rsquo;t win</p>
                <p><strong>Muddled in Their Thinking</strong></p>
                <p>Because the Outlaw doesn't like limitations, they want to be free without boundaries, and this leads to chaos.It&rsquo;s just the natural way of the world.&nbsp;</p>
                <p>Everything has to have some order and limitation (even nature has this pattern).But the Outlaw despises that (unless they&rsquo;ve already adjusted their habits to adapt socially), and so they become chaotic and muddled in their thinking which prevents them from achieving their goals and as we have already highlighted it is one of the main reasons why Outlaw&rsquo;s struggle to attain a sense of significance.&nbsp;</p>
                <p><strong>Outlaws Will Even Fight With The Limitations The Outlaw Needs To Put On Themselves</strong></p>
                <p>Outlaws even secretly fight with themselves.They will naturally have to have some limitation, but if they ever become conscious of the fact that they are limiting themselves, they&rsquo;ll probably try to switch things up which will lead to a chaotic lifestyle if the Outlaw doesn&rsquo;t become consciously aware of this pattern.</p>
                <p><strong>Outlaws Can Become Tired</strong></p>
                <p>Can you imagine living with the conflict and challenges that an Outlaw faces?Not just because of the way that they rebel against the things they need to do to reach their goals but also because they are so compassionate about the ways of the world. They are also continually sabotaging themselves all in the name of freedom.&nbsp;</p>
                <p>It&rsquo;s tiring thinking about it, let alone living it.&nbsp;</p>
                <p>If the Outlaw is not careful, they will burn themselves out.They need to address the conflict they feel inside to avoid exhausting themselves.&nbsp;</p>
                <p><strong>Outlaw&rsquo;s Don&rsquo;t Know How to Live Life Without Fighting For Freedom</strong></p>
                <p>An Outlaw will probably always have a fight for freedom on their hands. They cannot live without fighting for it.&nbsp;</p>
                <p>Sure, the world needs Outlaws to keep on bringing attention to the crazy and inhumane things that happen every day.But they secretly sacrifice so much for their plight (even they may not stop to think about this) that they don&rsquo;t get to enjoy life.&nbsp;</p>
                <p>If only they could recognize this and start to make some headway toward balancing it, then they&rsquo;d have more energy, connections, and inspiration to assist them with their projects.</p>
                <p><strong>Can Be Confused by Other People's Perspective and Boundaries (or lack thereof)</strong></p>
                <p>We all have a certain way of thinking that causes us to have put ourselves in another person's shoes to understand why they might think a certain way.But the Outlaw cannot easily understand why somebody is ok with some of the things that happen in life that they are not ok with.&nbsp;</p>
                <p>They see it as logical to think as they do (which it is in most cases) and cannot understand why others aren&rsquo;t as mad about things or motivated toward change as they are.&nbsp;</p>
                <p><strong>Outlaws Are Often Conflicted </strong></p>
                <p>As you can already see the Outlaw is hugely conflicted.They have to have limitation and order to create change but can&rsquo;t agree to that, and so they are permanently living in a &lsquo;checkmate&rsquo; situation.Again, this might be something they secretly fight against, or it is something that even they don&rsquo;t realize they are dealing with.</p>
                <p><strong>Outlaws Prioritize Change Over Anything That They Want to Do</strong></p>
                <p>Sometimes, and secretly an Outlaw will want to have a simple life. It's just that they can&rsquo;t have a simple life if there are limitations imposed that compromise on their freedom or the freedoms of others.It&rsquo;s quite the problem for an Outlaw.</p>
                <p><strong>Outlaws Are Good People</strong></p>
                <p>Outlaws get a bad rap.They may not want you to realize this because they probably like to look fierce, but they are often excellent people with big hearts who are just trying to make the world a better place.&nbsp;</p>
                <p>Not many people will get to see this secret side of an Outlaw though.&nbsp;</p>
                <p><strong>Outlaws Don&rsquo;t Want People to See Their Vulnerability</strong></p>
                <p>Outlaws are probably as afraid of being seen as vulnerable as they are about being limited.They have learned that they need to be strong to fight the battles.</p>
                <p>Outlaws are continually fighting and justifying themselves - and secretly they feel that if they show vulnerability, it could mean that others won&rsquo;t listen (even though they already have that problem generally anyway).They are also incredibly cynical and mistrusting which is another reason why they don't want you to see their vulnerability.</p>
                <p><strong>Have Trouble Expressing Themselves.</strong></p>
                <p>Because of the chaotic way that an Outlaw chooses to think and behave, the conflict that they experience, and the frustration they feel already, it&rsquo;s challenging for an Outlaw to express themselves.&nbsp;</p>
                <p>This problem makes them more frustrated and is something that they&rsquo;d benefit from admitting to themselves so that they can consciously work on creating a more fulfilling approach to how they express themselves.It might help them reach their goals much quicker.&nbsp;</p>
                <p><strong>It&rsquo;s An Intense Shadow World For The Outlaw</strong></p>
                <p>We weren&rsquo;t exaggerating when we said that the Outlaw has an intense shadow world.Their life is full of unexpressed desires, experiences, beliefs, and fears.It wouldn&rsquo;t be an exaggeration to say that the Outlaw is forced to live in their shadow world more than they do the real world.&nbsp;</p>
                <p><strong>Making them a true Outlaw of existence itself. </strong></p>
                <p>It is your destiny as an outlaw to feel the sense of accomplishment that real change can bring.But to attain this experience, you must first overcome the hurdles that are preventing you from getting there.It's easy to do this through the process of individuation and also through shadow work which will release you from the traps you have fallen into internally, and the pitfalls that restrict your freedom in the real world.&nbsp;</p>
